XQ={1:('How does the shared responsibility model change across IaaS, PaaS and SaaS, and how does that shape security design?','The customer always owns data, identities and endpoints. Moving from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS the provider takes on more of the OS, runtime and application. On IaaS you patch the OS and manage NSGs; on PaaS you focus on identity, data and configuration.','A team says Azure is down. How do you tell a platform issue from your own configuration?','Check Azure Service Health and Resource Health for platform events, review the Activity log for recent changes, then metrics and logs for the affected resource. If no platform event exists, treat it as configuration or application.','Why use regions and availability zones instead of one datacenter?','Zones isolate datacenter failures inside a region; regions give disaster recovery, lower latency to users and data residency. A single datacenter is a single point of failure.'),
2:('How would you design a management group hierarchy for 60 subscriptions?','Keep it shallow: root, then platform (identity, connectivity, management), landing zones (corp, online), sandbox and decommissioned. Apply Policy and RBAC at management groups so subscriptions inherit them.','A deployment fails with a resource provider error. What do you do?','Open Subscriptions, then Resource providers, register the needed provider (for example Microsoft.Network) with Contributor or Owner, and retry the deployment.','Why do resources belong to resource groups?','A group shares a lifecycle, and it is a scope for RBAC, Policy, tags and cost reporting. Deleting the group removes everything in it.'),
3:('A user is Owner but a deployment is denied. Why?','RBAC controls who can act; Policy evaluates the request separately. A Deny effect blocks it even for Owners. Read the error and Activity log, then fix the resource or request a policy exemption.','You assigned a role but the user still gets access denied. What next?','Allow a few minutes for propagation, have the user sign out and in to refresh the token, verify the scope, and check for deny assignments or policy.','Why assign roles to groups instead of users?','Groups make onboarding and offboarding simple, reduce the number of assignments, and improve auditability.'),
4:('When do you use a managed identity instead of a service principal, and how do you grant access?','Use it for Azure-hosted workloads so there is no secret to manage. Choose system-assigned for one resource or user-assigned to share, then assign a data-plane role at the narrowest scope.','Conditional Access locked users out after a change. How do you recover?','Sign in with an excluded break-glass account, switch the policy to Report-only or disable it, review the Conditional Access tab in sign-in logs, correct the assignments, and re-enable.','Why prefer Conditional Access MFA over per-user MFA?','It is centrally managed, context-aware, testable in report-only mode and scales better than legacy per-user settings.'),
5:('Design a hub-and-spoke network with centralized egress inspection.','Hub VNet holds Firewall, Bastion and VPN or ExpressRoute gateway; spokes peer to the hub with non-overlapping CIDR; UDR sends 0.0.0.0/0 to the firewall; private DNS zones link to the VNets; enable gateway transit.','A spoke VM cannot reach on-premises over VPN. How do you troubleshoot?','Verify peering settings for gateway transit and remote gateway, view Effective routes, check NSGs, confirm the VPN connection status and that on-premises routes include the spoke prefix.','Why use private endpoints instead of service endpoints for sensitive data?','A private endpoint gives a private IP, works from on-premises, targets one resource instance and lets you disable public access, which limits data exfiltration.'),
6:('How do you secure a storage account end to end?','Use Entra ID authentication, disable anonymous and shared-key access where possible, require TLS 1.2, restrict network access with firewall or private endpoint, enable soft delete and versioning, Defender for Storage and diagnostic logs.','Uploads suddenly fail with 403. What do you check?','Storage firewall and client IP, the data-plane role, SAS expiry and permissions, a rotated key, and the storage diagnostic logs for the exact error.','Why choose ZRS or GZRS over LRS in production?','LRS keeps copies in one datacenter. ZRS survives a zone failure and GZRS adds protection against a regional outage.'),
7:('How do you choose between VM, App Service, Functions and AKS?','Match control to operational effort: VMs for full OS control or lift-and-shift, App Service for web apps and APIs, Functions for event-driven code, AKS for containerized microservices that need orchestration.','A VM does not start after a resize or reboot. What do you check?','Boot diagnostics and Serial console, Resource Health, Activity log, vCPU quota and size availability in the zone, disk health; redeploy as a last resort.','Why use scale sets or zones instead of one large VM?','They add high availability, horizontal scale, rolling updates and fault isolation that one VM cannot provide.'),
8:('Design secret management for an app across dev, test and prod.','One Key Vault per environment with RBAC, managed identity for the app, no secrets in code, soft delete and purge protection, rotation, private endpoint and diagnostic logging.','Defender for Cloud raises a high-severity alert on a VM. First steps?','Validate the alert, isolate the VM with an NSG rule, capture evidence (snapshot and logs), investigate, rotate exposed credentials, remediate and document.','Why use JIT access and Bastion instead of public RDP or SSH?','They remove always-open management ports, cutting exposure to brute force and scanning.'),
9:('Design monitoring for a production web app.','Application Insights for app telemetry, diagnostic settings to Log Analytics, metric and log alerts routed by severity through action groups, availability tests and workbooks for dashboards.','You get an alert storm at 3 am. How do you cut the noise?','Tune severity and thresholds, use dynamic thresholds and aggregation, add alert processing rules, group notifications, and fix root causes.','Why centralize logs in Log Analytics?','It enables cross-resource KQL, correlation, retention control, alerting and SIEM integration.'),
10:('Meet an RPO of 15 minutes and an RTO of 1 hour. What is your strategy?','Daily backup alone is not enough. Add continuous replication (Site Recovery or database geo-replication), automated failover runbooks and regular drills, mapped to workload tiers.','Backups started failing after a lock was added. Why?','A lock on the resource group can block the Backup restore point collection. Remove it or follow Backup guidance, then run Backup now.','Why test restores and failovers regularly?','An untested backup is only an assumption. Drills find gaps and prove the RTO.'),
11:('How would you cut Azure spend by 30% without hurting reliability?','Use Cost analysis and Advisor to right-size or shut down idle resources, buy reservations or savings plans for steady load, tier storage, auto-shutdown dev/test, enforce tags and budgets.','Costs spiked this week. How do you find the cause?','Open Cost analysis at daily granularity grouped by service and resource, check anomaly alerts, compare to Activity log changes and scaling events.','Why tag resources for cost management?','Tags enable allocation and chargeback, ownership and automation.'),
12:('How do you manage Terraform state safely for a team?','Use a remote backend such as Azure Storage with state locking, RBAC on the account, soft delete and versioning, and separate state per environment.','terraform apply fails with a permission or provider error. What do you do?','Read the error, register the provider, check the pipeline identity roles, run plan again and import existing resources if needed.','Why use Infrastructure as Code instead of portal clicks?','It is repeatable, reviewable, auditable and detects drift.'),
13:('What are the design areas of an Azure landing zone?','Identity, network topology and connectivity, resource organization, security, management, governance, billing and platform automation.','A workload team needs a new subscription with guardrails. How is it provisioned?','Through subscription vending: the new subscription is placed in the right management group, inherits Policy and RBAC, and is connected to the network.','Why build a landing zone before deploying workloads?','It provides consistent guardrails and a scalable foundation so teams deploy safely without rework.')}
xq=''
for n,(aq,aa,pq,pa,wq,wa) in XQ.items():
    for lv,q,a in (('Advanced',aq,aa),('Production',pq,pa),('Why',wq,wa)):
        xq+=f'<details class="qa-item" data-lv="{lv}" data-p="{n}"><summary>{E(q)}</summary><div class="qa-answer"><p>{E(a)}</p></div></details>'
IQ={1:('What is the difference between IaaS, PaaS and SaaS, with an Azure example of each?','IaaS: you manage the OS and above (Virtual Machines). PaaS: the provider manages the platform, you manage app and data (App Service). SaaS: finished software (Microsoft 365).'),
2:('What is the difference between the control plane and the data plane?','The control plane manages resources through Azure Resource Manager (create, configure, delete). The data plane works with the contents (read a blob, query a database). Contributor does not grant blob data access.'),
3:('How do Azure RBAC role assignments inherit?','Assignments at a management group, subscription or resource group flow down to child scopes, so assign at the lowest scope that works.'),
4:('What is the difference between Microsoft Entra roles and Azure RBAC roles?','Entra roles manage directory objects such as users and apps; Azure RBAC roles manage Azure resources. They are separate systems.'),
5:('How is an NSG rule evaluated?','Rules are processed by priority from the lowest number up and the first match decides. Default rules allow VNet and outbound internet traffic and deny other inbound traffic.'),
6:('How do access tiers and lifecycle rules reduce storage cost?','Hot, Cool, Cold and Archive trade lower storage cost for higher access cost. Lifecycle rules move blobs by age automatically; Archive blobs need rehydration.'),
7:('How do Availability Sets and Availability Zones differ?','Availability Sets spread VMs across fault and update domains in one datacenter (99.95% SLA); Zones spread them across datacenters (99.99% SLA).'),
8:('What is the difference between Key Vault access policies and Azure RBAC?','Access policies are the legacy vault-level model; Azure RBAC is the recommended model with consistent roles, scopes and PIM support.'),
9:('What is the difference between metrics and logs?','Metrics are lightweight numeric time series for near-real-time alerts; logs are detailed event records queried with KQL.'),
10:('What is the difference between Azure Backup and Site Recovery?','Backup keeps recovery points so you can restore data; Site Recovery replicates workloads to another region for failover and continuity.'),
11:('How do Reservations, Savings Plans and Spot differ?','Reservations commit to specific resources for 1 or 3 years; Savings Plans commit to hourly spend with more flexibility; Spot uses spare capacity that can be evicted.'),
12:('What is the difference between terraform plan and terraform apply?','Plan previews changes by comparing configuration and state without changing anything; apply performs them.'),
13:('How does subscription vending work in a landing zone?','It automates creating subscriptions with baseline policy, RBAC, networking and tags so teams get compliant environments quickly.')}
for n,(q,a) in IQ.items():
    xq+=f'<details class="qa-item" data-lv="Intermediate" data-p="{n}"><summary>{E(q)}</summary><div class="qa-answer"><p>{E(a)}</p></div></details>'
